= Decision theory =

== Overview ==
'''Decision theory''' studies how choices can be represented, evaluated, or prescribed when an agent faces alternatives, consequences, and often uncertainty.

Normative decision theory asks what choices or preference structures satisfy stated rationality principles. Descriptive decision theory instead studies how people actually choose. The two programs interact but are not identical.

Many classical models represent options by possible outcomes, probabilities or beliefs about those outcomes, and a utility representation of how outcomes are ranked. Expected-utility theory is central, but it is not the only theory of rational choice.

A decision model is only as good as its state space, probabilities, utilities, and assumptions. Formal maximization does not by itself establish that the model captures all morally, socially, or practically relevant considerations.

== Boundaries ==
* decision theory ≠ psychology of choice by definition
* decision theory ≠ expected-utility theory only
* formal optimality ≠ factual or moral correctness

